\date{2025-08-12T21:38:26Z}
\title{Polarisation}
\author{vikraman}
\import{base-macros}

\p{
  Let #{\cat{L}} be a model of classical linear logic, with orthogonality #{\blank^\bot}, and a Lafont exponential (!).
  Let #{\cat{L}^{!}} and #{\cat{L}^{?}} be the categories of (co)algebras for #{!} and #{?}.
}

\p{
  #{\cat{L}^{!}} is a distributive category with an exponentiating object.
  #{\cat{L}^{?}} is a co-distributive category with a co-exponentiating object.
}

\p{
  The orthogonality on #{\cat{L}} lifts to an isomorphism #{{(\cat{L}^{!})}\op \natiso \cat{L}^{?}},
  and similarly #{{(\cat{L}^{?})}\op \natiso \cat{L}^{!}}.
}

\p{
  The exponentiating object in #{\cat{L}^{!}} induces a [continuation self-adjunction](0002).
  Composing the self-adjunction with the above isomorphism gives the shift adjunction between the two polarised categories
  #{\cat{L}^{!}} and #{\cat{L}^{?}}. Positive types are interpreted in #{\cat{L}^{!}} and negative types in #{\cat{L}^{?}}.
}

\p{
  Using this interpretation of [System L](0004), and writing the image of the interpretation in linear logic notation,
  one obtains the syntax of Polarised System L.
}
